  "explanation": "According to the cited reassessment, the narrative that COVID-19 transmission in the United States accelerated rapidly in March 2020 is not supported by the evidence. [1] Initial U.S. cases through late February were largely travel-related with limited community spread, indicating that transmission within the general population was constrained at that time. [1] Reports of community cases without clear links in late February were isolated and do not demonstrate a robust or accelerating transmission mechanism. [1] The increase in reported cases in mid-March primarily reflected expanded testing capacity and improved surveillance rather than a genuine surge in transmission. [1] Public health responses, including proactive case detection, contact tracing, and mitigation measures, were implemented early, and most of the population remained unexposed during this period. [1] Therefore, the perceived March acceleration was driven by detection and awareness effects rather than an actual speed-up of transmission. [1]"
}

